WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.534 --> 00:00:05.234
-I was told my hat
looked weird

00:00:05.234 --> 00:00:06.901
because I was wearing it
too low.

00:00:10.834 --> 00:00:13.901
There is a French town

00:00:13.901 --> 00:00:17.334
that hosts
a very famous film festival.

00:00:17.334 --> 00:00:21.934
I never quite know
how to pronounce it.

00:00:21.934 --> 00:00:23.767
I've been getting
a lot of feedback on it.

00:00:23.767 --> 00:00:28.634
I've said, "Can,"
"cans," "cons."

00:00:28.634 --> 00:00:32.667
My mother, who we've established
over the years,

00:00:32.667 --> 00:00:33.968
is a French teacher,

00:00:33.968 --> 00:00:37.701
so I realized
I could reach out to her.

00:00:37.701 --> 00:00:39.634
I said, "Will you
please record yourself

00:00:39.634 --> 00:00:44.934
giving the proper pronunciation
of this French town

00:00:44.934 --> 00:00:46.501
that hosts a film festival?"

00:00:46.501 --> 00:00:48.934
Now, keep in mind,

00:00:48.934 --> 00:00:52.801
Hillary is not great
with technology.

00:00:52.801 --> 00:00:56.734
But I asked her to record it
and and she did.

00:00:56.734 --> 00:01:00.367
So this is how she says
you pronounce this town.

00:01:00.367 --> 00:01:01.467
Let's take a listen.

00:01:01.467 --> 00:01:03.834
-Khan!

00:01:03.834 --> 00:01:06.033
[ Laughter ]

00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:14.067
-[ Clears throat ]

00:01:16.901 --> 00:01:20.901
I referenced the movie "Air Bud"

00:01:20.901 --> 00:01:22.968
because we were talking
about ear buds and AirPods.

00:01:22.968 --> 00:01:26.801
And I mentioned "Air Bud,"
basketball-playing dog.

00:01:26.801 --> 00:01:30.701
Someone said "Air Bud"
didn't play basketball.

00:01:30.701 --> 00:01:33.133
He played football.

00:01:33.133 --> 00:01:34.767
You're wrong.

00:01:37.501 --> 00:01:39.334
You made a mistake, friend.

00:01:39.334 --> 00:01:44.000
Or to put it another way,
you aired, bud.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.334
[ Light laughter, groans ]

00:01:45.334 --> 00:01:47.834
If you don't like wordplay...

00:01:47.834 --> 00:01:50.367
[ Laughter ]

00:01:52.234 --> 00:01:55.033
Um...he did play football
in the second --

00:01:55.033 --> 00:01:56.934
There were multiple
"Air Bud" films.

00:01:56.934 --> 00:01:59.901
He did play -- The second fil--

00:01:59.901 --> 00:02:03.234
It was "Air Bud,"
"Air Bud: Golden Receiver,"

00:02:03.234 --> 00:02:05.667
"Air Bud: World Pup,"

00:02:05.667 --> 00:02:09.734
"Air Bud: Seventh Inning Fetch,"

00:02:09.734 --> 00:02:11.801
"Air Bud: Spikes Back."

00:02:11.801 --> 00:02:14.501
This is when the critics started
to turn...

00:02:14.501 --> 00:02:16.934
[ Laughter ]

00:02:16.934 --> 00:02:20.334
...after "Air Bud: Spikes Back."

00:02:20.334 --> 00:02:23.267
Some -- not all --
some of the critics

00:02:23.267 --> 00:02:27.367
said the franchise was losing
its creative luster.

00:02:28.834 --> 00:02:30.367
This is cool because
we have talked about it.

00:02:30.367 --> 00:02:32.000
Obviously, we talked about
Lassie on the show

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:33.734
that obviously
the same dog can't play,

00:02:33.734 --> 00:02:35.634
you know, due to life spans,
can't play --

00:02:35.634 --> 00:02:37.000
They have to replace the dog
at some point.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:40.534
That's just, you know,
the circle of life.

00:02:40.534 --> 00:02:41.701
This is cool.

00:02:41.701 --> 00:02:44.067
The dog, whose name
actually Air Buddy,

00:02:44.067 --> 00:02:48.267
was in seven of the movies
before he passed away.

00:02:48.267 --> 00:02:51.300
Oh.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:52.767
I read that in dog movies.

00:02:52.767 --> 00:02:54.400
He was only in one.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:55.868
[ Laughter ]

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.167
This is...

00:03:05.167 --> 00:03:08.400
So this is the crazy thing.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.267
[ Laughs ] John...
A lot of people in the comm--

00:03:10.267 --> 00:03:13.567
John Oliver did
a long piece on "Air Bud,"

00:03:13.567 --> 00:03:15.501
like, a few weeks ago.

00:03:15.501 --> 00:03:17.734
It was online only.

00:03:17.734 --> 00:03:18.767
It was fantastic.

00:03:18.767 --> 00:03:20.801
I highly recommend
you go and watch

00:03:20.801 --> 00:03:26.601
our friend John's piece
on "Air Bud" that he did.

00:03:26.601 --> 00:03:28.634
Again, like I said,
online only.

00:03:28.634 --> 00:03:32.400
It was 15 minutes.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.634
Can you imagine?

00:03:35.634 --> 00:03:38.767
Not for TV.

00:03:38.767 --> 00:03:42.934
15 minutes for a digital only.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:52.100
Does no one...

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.300
respect the audience's time?

00:03:57.834 --> 00:04:01.767
Back...to corrections.

00:04:03.567 --> 00:04:06.634
Some other news from
our late-night colleagues.

00:04:08.567 --> 00:04:09.634
There you go.

00:04:12.501 --> 00:04:15.467
Someone wrote,
"This whole show..."

00:04:19.467 --> 00:04:21.467
Someone wrote in the comments,
"This whole show

00:04:21.467 --> 00:04:26.334
is getting less and less
entertaining."

00:04:26.334 --> 00:04:32.300
I think you mean fewer
and fewer entertaining.

00:04:33.667 --> 00:04:36.701
Because if I've learned
anything,

00:04:36.701 --> 00:04:38.701
it's always the opposite.

00:04:42.934 --> 00:04:46.133
So, um,
a lot of you noticed

00:04:46.133 --> 00:04:50.033
that, um, I left the cardboard
in my hat.

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:53.801
[ Speaks indistinctly ]

00:04:53.801 --> 00:04:57.300
And, uh, the crew did, too.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:58.834
And pretty much as soon
as I walked off,

00:04:58.834 --> 00:05:02.334
everybody said, uh...

00:05:02.334 --> 00:05:04.400
"I guess now you finally know
why you have Donna around."

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:07.300
Donna is -- is my dresser.

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:11.033
Donna --
also the dresser for "SNL."

00:05:11.033 --> 00:05:12.200
Best in the business.

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:14.567
We have a lot of people here
who do jobs here,

00:05:14.567 --> 00:05:15.968
and job at "SNL."

00:05:15.968 --> 00:05:18.200
Donna -- dresser here,
dresser there.

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:19.467
Wally -- obviously,
cue cards here,

00:05:19.467 --> 00:05:21.434
cue cards there.

00:05:21.434 --> 00:05:23.801
Lorne Michaels does night crew
for both shows.

00:05:23.801 --> 00:05:25.701
[ Laughter ]

00:05:25.701 --> 00:05:28.434
I mean, I think the craziest
thing is as soon as --

00:05:28.434 --> 00:05:31.267
you know, his suit and tie
and then...

00:05:31.267 --> 00:05:33.934
just puts on his little
carpenter outfit.

00:05:37.534 --> 00:05:39.667
So everybody said,
"Oh, Donna would have known

00:05:39.667 --> 00:05:40.901
to take the cardboard out."

00:05:40.901 --> 00:05:44.868
And I would never
run afoul of Donna.

00:05:44.868 --> 00:05:46.567
Donna is a very
important person.

00:05:46.567 --> 00:05:50.033
And incredibly, I think
you would attest to this,

00:05:50.033 --> 00:05:51.167
vindictive.

00:05:51.167 --> 00:05:52.200
I, um...

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:53.901
[ Laughter ]

00:05:53.901 --> 00:05:56.033
I once accidentally --
I accidentally

00:05:56.033 --> 00:05:58.734
was a little bit --
a little bit short with Donna.

00:05:58.734 --> 00:06:01.734
And after the show,

00:06:01.734 --> 00:06:04.467
she super-glued
the zipper in my pants.

00:06:06.868 --> 00:06:08.434
And she does it in a way
you don't know

00:06:08.434 --> 00:06:10.501
when you pull them on.

00:06:10.501 --> 00:06:12.167
It's only when you when
you zip them up,

00:06:12.167 --> 00:06:13.601
that activates the glue.

00:06:13.601 --> 00:06:15.968
And so then it's only later when
you got to go to the bathroom

00:06:15.968 --> 00:06:18.033
and you got to, like, pull
them -- like, it's like --

00:06:18.033 --> 00:06:20.133
I'm like a 5-year-old
at a urinal.

00:06:20.133 --> 00:06:23.701
Remember when that time
she kept --

00:06:23.701 --> 00:06:24.767
She did this other thing.

00:06:24.767 --> 00:06:27.367
She got someone to hem my pants,

00:06:27.367 --> 00:06:31.801
like, a 16th of an inch
every day for a month.

00:06:31.801 --> 00:06:33.701
Remember when I came
to your office and I was like,

00:06:33.701 --> 00:06:36.968
"I got to go to the E.R.

00:06:36.968 --> 00:06:38.567
I'm growing."

00:06:41.033 --> 00:06:43.467
So I would never run afoul
of Donna.

00:06:43.467 --> 00:06:45.801
With that said,
I know if I wanted

00:06:45.801 --> 00:06:48.634
to take the cardboard
out of my hat,

00:06:48.634 --> 00:06:50.801
that's within my...

00:06:50.801 --> 00:06:52.400
I mean, it's...

00:07:01.267 --> 00:07:03.834
Or I would just be like,
"That's the way I wanted it."

00:07:05.868 --> 00:07:07.634
Hey, we in --

00:07:07.634 --> 00:07:10.234
Just a couple weeks ago,
we went after

00:07:10.234 --> 00:07:13.000
people who post
their Wordle scores

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:16.100
on Twitter.

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:20.868
And a lot of you --
also pretty vindictive --

00:07:20.868 --> 00:07:23.534
have now been
just tweeting them at me.

00:07:25.834 --> 00:07:27.567
And I just want to say
that everybody

00:07:27.567 --> 00:07:30.734
who has shared your Wordle
scores with me,

00:07:30.734 --> 00:07:32.400
I'm so proud of you.

00:07:38.434 --> 00:07:42.634
So I was talking about
Sir Arthur Conan Doyle,

00:07:42.634 --> 00:07:45.534
and I referenced the fact

00:07:45.534 --> 00:07:46.834
that Shoemaker
is the kind of guy

00:07:46.834 --> 00:07:48.234
who drops the "sir"

00:07:48.234 --> 00:07:50.267
because he has no respect
for the Crown.

00:07:50.267 --> 00:07:56.067
And then I was told
it is a living title.

00:07:56.067 --> 00:07:57.467
You don't keep it.

00:07:57.467 --> 00:08:01.734
The minute he dropped dead,
there went the "sir."

00:08:01.734 --> 00:08:03.300
And I was fascinated by this.

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:05.601
And then I read more because,
again, part of corrections

00:08:05.601 --> 00:08:07.434
is you guys send me
down a road,

00:08:07.434 --> 00:08:10.400
and then I do a great deal
of research to educate myself.

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:14.834
They bring the body
to the Queen,

00:08:14.834 --> 00:08:17.367
who takes a sword,
puts it on their shoulder

00:08:17.367 --> 00:08:18.734
and goes...

00:08:24.167 --> 00:08:26.400
We showed a CAT scan
instead of an X-ray.

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:29.133
We showed a Quarter Pounder
instead of a Big Mac.

00:08:29.133 --> 00:08:32.234
So I wrote --
This is me.

00:08:32.234 --> 00:08:34.534
I wrote a joke about, like,
the fact that senators

00:08:34.534 --> 00:08:35.767
don't actually --
They say they want

00:08:35.767 --> 00:08:36.934
to do something
about gun control

00:08:36.934 --> 00:08:38.701
and they don't do anything.

00:08:38.701 --> 00:08:41.000
And it would be like
a spouse saying,

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:43.267
"I'll help with the dishes,
but I don't want to get on --

00:08:43.267 --> 00:08:45.367
I don't want to get
off the couch.

00:08:45.367 --> 00:08:47.100
I'm just going
to attach this --"

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:48.968
This is what I wrote.

00:08:48.968 --> 00:08:51.067
"I'll just attach a sponge
to a long stick."

00:08:51.067 --> 00:08:53.133
Now, in my head,
what I pictured

00:08:53.133 --> 00:08:56.267
was a long stick
with a sponge on it

00:08:56.267 --> 00:08:58.100
so you could still do something.

00:08:58.100 --> 00:08:59.901
This is what graphics --

00:08:59.901 --> 00:09:02.167
I guess this is what
they thought I meant.

00:09:02.167 --> 00:09:03.734
[ Laughter ]

00:09:03.734 --> 00:09:09.133
I'm not saying my idea was good,
but this is idiotic.

00:09:09.133 --> 00:09:10.801
[ Laughter ]

00:09:15.067 --> 00:09:16.968
Alright.

00:09:16.968 --> 00:09:18.400
We -- Let's see.

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:20.501
We had a shot...

00:09:22.567 --> 00:09:25.934
...of Rudy Giuliani is, like,
self-incriminating all the time.

00:09:25.934 --> 00:09:27.901
And we get a shot of his phone
and we said,

00:09:27.901 --> 00:09:29.400
"Let's zoom in on that phone.

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:32.601
And that's how
we could prove his guilt."

00:09:32.601 --> 00:09:37.367
And on the phone it said,
"I'm guilty."

00:09:37.367 --> 00:09:39.167
But a bunch of you pointed out

00:09:39.167 --> 00:09:42.000
that was a received message
on his phone.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:45.367
That was not Rudy Giuliani
telling someone, "I'm guilty."

00:09:45.367 --> 00:09:48.100
That was someone
telling Rudy Giuliani,

00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:49.234
"I'm guilty."

00:09:49.234 --> 00:09:53.534
But we had forgot
to put his outgoing text

00:09:53.534 --> 00:09:56.234
to his lawyer, which was...

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:58.334
There you go.

00:09:58.334 --> 00:09:59.667
[ Light laughter ]

00:10:03.868 --> 00:10:05.801
Ooh, is it --

00:10:11.767 --> 00:10:16.167
15 minutes on "Air Bud."

00:10:16.167 --> 00:10:17.234
I love the guy.

00:10:19.467 --> 00:10:21.200
The crew --
He kept the crew there.

00:10:23.767 --> 00:10:26.567
Like they have -- like
they don't have kids.

00:10:29.534 --> 00:10:30.767
We said Disneyland

00:10:30.767 --> 00:10:33.767
but showed a picture of Epcot
at Disney World.

00:10:33.767 --> 00:10:34.801
A lot of you pointed that out.

00:10:34.801 --> 00:10:37.067
A lot of you.

00:10:37.067 --> 00:10:38.234
One of you went so far to say

00:10:38.234 --> 00:10:41.100
we showed a picture
of the pink monorail

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:43.801
that had been discontinued
in 2009.

00:10:43.801 --> 00:10:45.801
We did do those on purpose.

00:10:45.801 --> 00:10:48.868
We have an arrangement
with a government agency

00:10:48.868 --> 00:10:52.133
wherein we put mistakes
about Disney,

00:10:52.133 --> 00:10:56.167
the Disney properties,
and if adults correct us,

00:10:56.167 --> 00:10:58.801
we turn those names over
and you're put on a list.

00:10:58.801 --> 00:11:01.801
[ Laughter ]

00:11:01.801 --> 00:11:04.868
No one's saying
you committed a crime.

00:11:04.868 --> 00:11:08.100
It's a watch list.

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:09.801
We're just gonna watch it now.

00:11:14.534 --> 00:11:17.701
We talked about
our new sponsor --

00:11:17.701 --> 00:11:21.501
[ German accent ]
Werner's Original.

00:11:21.501 --> 00:11:23.667
Original.

00:11:23.667 --> 00:11:25.000
[ Normal voice ]
Some Germans tried to tell me

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:25.834
I was saying "original" wrong.

00:11:25.834 --> 00:11:28.200
And I got some criticism,

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:32.868
a very helpful
impression note,

00:11:32.868 --> 00:11:36.234
which is that
a good Werner Herzog

00:11:36.234 --> 00:11:40.767
should have "whispered tone
and desperate curiosity."

00:11:44.434 --> 00:11:46.100
[ German accent ]
Werner's Originals

00:11:46.100 --> 00:11:49.467
are hard candy
within which lies mystery.

00:11:49.467 --> 00:11:52.901
Together, let's take the
confectionery journey together.

00:11:52.901 --> 00:11:56.234
And while we might not find
the answers we're looking for,

00:11:56.234 --> 00:11:57.334
Is it not possible

00:11:57.334 --> 00:12:00.801
we will learn something
about ourselves?

00:12:07.133 --> 00:12:08.300
[ Normal voice ]
This is a terrible joke

00:12:08.300 --> 00:12:11.267
that I decided against.

00:12:11.267 --> 00:12:12.501
The rival candy

00:12:12.501 --> 00:12:15.000
was Klaus Kinski's
Fitz-Caramel-Dos.

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:18.767
[ Laughter ]

00:12:18.767 --> 00:12:21.501
But, uh...

00:12:21.501 --> 00:12:23.701
I thought better of it.

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:29.267
Hey, I gave the Burnt Boys,
the writers of "Ya Burnt,"

00:12:29.267 --> 00:12:31.934
I gave them a little grief
last week.

00:12:31.934 --> 00:12:33.901
A lot of what they did
was, in fact, wrong,

00:12:33.901 --> 00:12:37.968
but I said they shouldn't have
put an apostrophe "S" after CDs.

00:12:37.968 --> 00:12:39.267
I was told by an English major

00:12:39.267 --> 00:12:41.367
that you're allowed
to put apostrophe "S",

00:12:41.367 --> 00:12:44.567
a possessive apostrophe
after acronyms.

00:12:44.567 --> 00:12:47.300
So I felt terrible.

00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:51.000
And I went -- I went down
to the Burnt Boys' offices

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:54.033
to apologize, forgetting that

00:12:54.033 --> 00:12:55.300
after they do a "Ya Burnt,"

00:12:55.300 --> 00:12:57.234
they give themselves
the next week off.

00:12:57.234 --> 00:12:59.234
[ Laughter ]

00:13:05.467 --> 00:13:08.701
We said Wally had a series
of "Where's Waldo" books

00:13:08.701 --> 00:13:09.901
called "Where's Wally?"

00:13:09.901 --> 00:13:12.501
Turns out that's what
the is called in England.

00:13:12.501 --> 00:13:13.467
It's "Where's Wally?"

00:13:13.467 --> 00:13:14.968
I guess, for whatever reason,

00:13:14.968 --> 00:13:17.834
Americans thought "Waldo"
would work better.

00:13:17.834 --> 00:13:21.367
That's a lot -- It's "Wally"
in a lot of places.

00:13:21.367 --> 00:13:24.400
Brazil, Australia, Ireland,
all Wally's.

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:26.334
The Netherlands.

00:13:26.334 --> 00:13:28.734
In Sweden, it's Hugo.

00:13:28.734 --> 00:13:29.968
Of course, it's not
"Where's Hugo?"

00:13:29.968 --> 00:13:31.367
because that's English.

00:13:31.367 --> 00:13:33.367
It's [Swedish accent]
"Where's Hugo?"

00:13:33.367 --> 00:13:35.167
[ Laughter ]

00:13:43.634 --> 00:13:44.968
[ Normal voice ]
You know, we don't get

00:13:44.968 --> 00:13:48.601
a lot of fan art
at our show,

00:13:48.601 --> 00:13:51.801
but...

00:13:51.801 --> 00:13:55.100
We had a really nice,
authentic moment with Wally.

00:13:55.100 --> 00:13:56.334
Wally's still here.

00:13:56.334 --> 00:13:58.968
Where I made a joke
about New Jersey.

00:13:58.968 --> 00:14:02.267
How shocking it is
that New Jersey had a tree.

00:14:02.267 --> 00:14:04.968
Wally scoffed.

00:14:04.968 --> 00:14:06.501
And I, during the monologue,

00:14:06.501 --> 00:14:08.467
asked Wally
to explain his scoff,

00:14:08.467 --> 00:14:09.634
and he told us proudly

00:14:09.634 --> 00:14:12.701
that he knows there's
a tree in New Jersey

00:14:12.701 --> 00:14:14.167
because he's got two.

00:14:18.067 --> 00:14:20.033
And then someone made this,
which is --

00:14:20.033 --> 00:14:21.200
I thought this is really great,

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:23.400
which is "Meet
Your New Jersey Mobsters."

